205
00:19:52,340 --> 00:19:56,900
Interesting choice that the writer chose to say, no shame.

206
00:19:56,900 --> 00:20:03,440
Where they could have said things like, hold on, I jumped ahead.

207
00:20:03,960 --> 00:20:07,640
This is the author's one-line summary of the state of humanity before sin.

208
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:11,460
Two people utterly naked before each other with no shame.

209
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:14,000
A Hebrew scholar says it this way.

210
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:22,240
The ideal picture is one of relational safety, vulnerability, and trust and acceptance of one another.

211
00:20:22,240 --> 00:20:28,400
And this goes all the way with sin, and it's in place comes shame.

212
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:34,220
Think about how fascinating the author chose to use this word.

213
00:20:34,220 --> 00:20:43,380
They could have said, no fear, no anger, no hate, no sadness, no despair.

214
00:20:43,380 --> 00:20:49,280
Because all those words sort of fit in the grander word of shame.

215
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:53,960
But instead, Moses chose to use the word shame.

216
00:20:53,960 --> 00:21:00,300
Shame isn't just feeling bad about yourself because you did something wrong.

217
00:21:00,300 --> 00:21:04,840
No, it's deeper fear that you are unlovable.

218
00:21:04,840 --> 00:21:09,400
It's not just because you did something wrong because you were bad and your parent,

219
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:13,040
your dad's coming home from work, and you're going to get in trouble today, okay?

220
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:17,400
That's just general fear because you did something stupid maybe, all right?

221
00:21:17,400 --> 00:21:22,960
And your dad's going to, you know, get some retributive justice going on in your house.

222
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:29,660
Shame speaks to an unlovable peace that is deep within you,

223
00:21:29,660 --> 00:21:33,380
that somebody would reject you and even cast you out.

224
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:35,400
That's why it feels so scary.

225
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:36,860
That's why it feels so scary.

226
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:39,180
Our fear isn't just that we were bad.

227
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:43,140
It's that we lose connection to those we love.

228
00:21:43,140 --> 00:21:46,720
And this fear isn't based on fantasy.

229
00:21:46,720 --> 00:21:48,660
It's based on the reality of sin.

230
00:21:48,660 --> 00:21:55,160
Once sin entered the human story, one of the results is ruined relationship.

231
00:21:55,160 --> 00:21:59,620
Sin is primarily about relational rupture.

232
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:05,000
And not only does sin disconnect Adam and Eve from God,

233
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,800
but it disconnected them from each other.

234
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:10,440
They blamed each other immediately.

235
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:13,900
And then out of that, a broken marriage.

236
00:22:13,900 --> 00:22:17,220
Their children, one killed the other one.

237
00:22:17,220 --> 00:22:22,700
And then from there, it just spills out into the wider society,

238
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:25,800
almost all the way into Genesis 5,

239
00:22:25,800 --> 00:22:29,140
where the writer says that when Seth was born,

240
00:22:29,140 --> 00:22:31,660
he was created in the image of his father,

241
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:33,820
which is interesting note.

242
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:39,520
Seth was created in the image of Adam because sin had entered into the world.

243
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:42,860
We often hear that this story is about sin and it is,

244
00:22:42,860 --> 00:22:45,360
but it's about a brokenness.

245
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:51,080
It is about sin's shadow of shame that came upon Adam and Eve.

246
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:55,380
What's the first thing that Adam and Eve did after they sinned?

247
00:22:55,380 --> 00:22:58,360
They hid themselves.

248
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:03,040
They hid themselves from God.

249
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:06,680
God even made them coverings for their nakedness

250
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:11,620
because they were ashamed of the very way that God created them.

251
00:23:11,860 --> 00:23:16,460
But yet he loved them so much that he created something for them

252
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:20,320
to cover up the shame that they were even carrying.

253
00:23:20,320 --> 00:23:25,940
Instead of moving towards God and towards each other,

254
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:30,160
they moved away and hid themselves from the Lord.

255
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:39,220
This speaks to a very deeper thing that we would love for the world to be rid of,

256
00:23:39,220 --> 00:23:41,080
but unfortunately it's not.

257
00:23:41,080 --> 00:23:45,580
We still come face to face with it all the time.

258
00:23:45,580 --> 00:23:49,680
Sin is what caused us to reject another person.

259
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:56,500
But the shame came over our sin is what causes us to hide from them

260
00:23:56,500 --> 00:23:58,580
out of fear that they'll reject us.

261
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:00,280
Shame is a disconnector.

262
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:05,620
It makes us hide from each other, blame each other, and wound each other.

263
00:24:05,620 --> 00:24:09,760
It makes us hide from God and even hide from ourselves.

264
00:24:09,760 --> 00:24:17,660
Shame is not only a byproduct of sin, it often causes sin.

265
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:20,420
Many of us don't realize this,

266
00:24:20,420 --> 00:24:22,800
but our dysfunctional behavior and relationships

267
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:26,420
can be caused by shame that we're carrying.

268
00:24:26,420 --> 00:24:29,600
Sit with that for a second.

269
00:24:29,700 --> 00:24:33,580
Like how many of us have dysfunctional relationships that we've entered into

270
00:24:33,580 --> 00:24:38,520
and we're sort of the byproduct of the thing that we're carrying with us

271
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:40,700
that has been the thing in the relationship

272
00:24:40,700 --> 00:24:44,060
that causes a pain or a suffering to happen in it?

273
00:24:44,060 --> 00:24:45,240
It's okay.

274
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:47,680
I mean, I don't want to say like it's okay,

275
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:49,540
like to make an excuse for it,

276
00:24:49,540 --> 00:24:51,920
but it's okay to call the thing out for what it is.

277
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:53,860
We have to be able to do that.

278
00:24:53,860 --> 00:24:56,840
We are constantly self-sabotaging ourselves.

279
00:24:56,840 --> 00:25:02,900
And we all have a genuine desire for love and intimacy,

280
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:05,580
but yet we do sabotage ourselves.

281
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:09,140
Some psychologists call this a self-betrayal,

282
00:25:09,140 --> 00:25:14,840
the way that we provoke others to behave in this very behavior of hate.

283
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:19,460
But it's shame, our ancient enemy,

284
00:25:19,460 --> 00:25:22,300
that's wreaking havoc against us.

285
00:25:22,300 --> 00:25:26,700
Let's turn to Romans chapter 7, verse 21 through 25.

286
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:28,120
And you don't need to stand.

287
00:25:28,120 --> 00:25:29,100
We're just going to read it.

288
00:25:29,100 --> 00:25:32,620
And Paul says here, starting in verse 21,

289
00:25:32,620 --> 00:25:36,980
I find then the principle that evil is present in me,

290
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:39,220
the one who wants to do good.

291
00:25:39,220 --> 00:25:43,440
For I joyfully concur with the law of God in the inner man.

292
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:45,500
But I see a different law,

293
00:25:45,500 --> 00:25:50,120
the members of my body waging war against the law of my mind,

294
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:52,960
making me a prisoner of the law of sin,

295
00:25:52,960 --> 00:25:56,500
which is in my members, speaking of his body.

296
00:25:56,500 --> 00:25:58,700
Wretched man that I am,

297
00:25:58,700 --> 00:26:02,400
who will set me free from this body of death?

298
00:26:02,940 --> 00:26:05,420
Thanks be to God through Jesus Christ, our Lord.

299
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:08,020
So then on one hand,

300
00:26:08,020 --> 00:26:12,320
I myself with my mind am serving the law of God.

301
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:13,800
But on the other hand,

302
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:16,980
with my flesh, the law of sin.

303
00:26:16,980 --> 00:26:18,640
He doesn't stop there though.

304
00:26:18,640 --> 00:26:19,960
He goes to chapter 8.

305
00:26:19,960 --> 00:26:22,920
Therefore, there is no condemnation

306
00:26:22,920 --> 00:26:25,120
for those who are in Christ Jesus.

307
00:26:25,120 --> 00:26:26,640
We read that this morning.

308
00:26:26,640 --> 00:26:31,900
For the law of the spirit of life in Christ Jesus

309
00:26:31,900 --> 00:26:35,160
has set you free from the law of sin and death.

310
00:26:35,160 --> 00:26:36,920
This is the good news.

311
00:26:36,920 --> 00:26:40,580
This is why we continue to read

312
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:44,980
and we don't just stop and try to create theology

313
00:26:44,980 --> 00:26:47,880
off of something that's just,

314
00:26:47,880 --> 00:26:49,940
off of just chapter 7.

315
00:26:49,940 --> 00:26:53,140
We'll severely hinder our understanding

316
00:26:53,140 --> 00:26:55,680
of what Paul is trying to get across

317
00:26:55,680 --> 00:26:58,880
if we were to have stopped at verse 25.

318
00:26:59,140 --> 00:27:01,540
If we don't go into verse 8,

319
00:27:01,540 --> 00:27:04,060
a chapter in chapter 8 verse 1,

320
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:08,520
we would miss what Paul is trying to bring us into.

321
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:11,140
He's showing us something in chapter 7

322
00:27:11,140 --> 00:27:14,200
and bringing us into something in chapter 8,

323
00:27:14,200 --> 00:27:14,720
saying,

324
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:25,920
He's speaking about this shame that they felt in the garden.

325
00:27:25,920 --> 00:27:28,920
He's speaking about this thing that he has no idea

326
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:29,880
that he's wrestling with,

327
00:27:29,880 --> 00:27:32,400
yet it's ever present in him.

328
00:27:32,820 --> 00:27:35,300
And how does he get through it?

329
00:27:35,300 --> 00:27:40,120
Only by the presence of Christ that is alive in him,

330
00:27:40,120 --> 00:27:41,560
but it's not just for him.

331
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:43,800
It's for all of us that are,

332
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:45,700
that have Christ.

333
00:27:45,700 --> 00:27:48,280
For the law of the spirit of Jesus Christ

334
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:51,720
has set you free from the law of sin and death.

335
00:27:53,680 --> 00:27:56,780
Jesus the Messiah has come to set you free

336
00:27:56,780 --> 00:28:01,140
and free from sin and its results of shame

337
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:03,020
to reconnect us to God

338
00:28:03,020 --> 00:28:06,940
and reconnect us to others in the thick bond of love.

339
00:28:06,940 --> 00:28:10,000
This raises the question,

340
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,060
how do we do it?

341
00:28:12,060 --> 00:28:14,380
This sounds really easy.

342
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:16,240
Just accept it and go on from here.

343
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:17,760
We can go have lunch.

344
00:28:18,340 --> 00:28:19,020
You guys are getting,

345
00:28:19,020 --> 00:28:20,120
we'll be done here soon.

346
00:28:20,120 --> 00:28:23,300
You can probably beat the charismatics to lunch.

347
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:26,300
The Pentecostals are still worshiping.

348
00:28:26,300 --> 00:28:30,280
It sounds nice and pious to say

349
00:28:30,280 --> 00:28:33,740
that Jesus is the answer to the problem of sin and shame.

350
00:28:33,740 --> 00:28:36,820
But how many of us love Jesus deeply

351
00:28:36,820 --> 00:28:38,640
and have been following him for years,

352
00:28:38,640 --> 00:28:41,380
yet we still struggle with sin?

353
00:28:41,380 --> 00:28:45,520
We hear there is no condemnation,

354
00:28:45,520 --> 00:28:47,380
yet we feel shame.

355
00:28:48,340 --> 00:28:51,020
Our parents couldn't stay married.

356
00:28:51,020 --> 00:28:54,240
Our community couldn't stay together.

357
00:28:54,240 --> 00:28:57,460
Our pastor couldn't even stay faithful.

358
00:28:57,460 --> 00:29:02,200
How does the gospel of Jesus become a reality

359
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:04,860
and not just in our minds,

360
00:29:04,860 --> 00:29:06,780
but also in our bodies?

361
00:29:06,780 --> 00:29:11,640
Well, if anyone tells you that it's easy,

362
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:13,680
they're selling you a bill of goods

363
00:29:13,680 --> 00:29:15,440
that you should return.

364
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:18,620
The wound goes deep.

365
00:29:18,620 --> 00:29:23,540
Relationships expose the raw edges of our souls

366
00:29:23,540 --> 00:29:27,640
where we all are in a process of walking through things.

367
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:29,300
Living in community,

368
00:29:29,300 --> 00:29:30,700
God gently,

369
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:36,200
but ever so slowly unearths layer after layer,

370
00:29:36,200 --> 00:29:39,400
allowing these shadow sides of ourselves

371
00:29:39,400 --> 00:29:40,400
to get exposed.

372
00:29:41,120 --> 00:29:42,680
So we deal with it.

373
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:44,880
It has to.

374
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:47,320
The only way to deal with it

375
00:29:47,320 --> 00:29:52,140
is the long journey of life in community

376
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:56,080
that allows you to come face to face with the things

377
00:29:56,080 --> 00:29:58,560
so that you can deal with it.

378
00:29:59,240 --> 00:30:00,160
It's painful.

379
00:30:00,160 --> 00:30:00,240
It's painful.

380
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:01,580
It's not easy.

381
00:30:01,580 --> 00:30:03,540
But it's a good thing.

382
00:30:03,540 --> 00:30:06,480
There's painful parts of our lives

383
00:30:06,480 --> 00:30:10,680
that it would be much easier not to face

384
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:12,500
or if they're not exposed,

385
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:14,080
but it's better to,

386
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:15,560
if we allow ourselves

387
00:30:15,560 --> 00:30:18,100
to be formed into the image of Christ,

388
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:22,540
not deformed by running from problems,

389
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:25,220
but being like bison when a storm comes.

390
00:30:25,800 --> 00:30:27,320
Bison walk into the storm,

391
00:30:27,320 --> 00:30:29,140
they don't run from a storm,

392
00:30:29,140 --> 00:30:31,440
meaning that they know

393
00:30:31,440 --> 00:30:32,820
when they're walking into the storm,

394
00:30:32,820 --> 00:30:34,860
they're gonna get through to the other side

395
00:30:34,860 --> 00:30:37,040
because the worst part is right there.

396
00:30:37,040 --> 00:30:39,200
In many ways,

397
00:30:39,200 --> 00:30:40,880
this is our spiritual journey

398
00:30:40,880 --> 00:30:42,960
that we take one step at a time,

399
00:30:42,960 --> 00:30:44,780
one layer at a time,

400
00:30:44,780 --> 00:30:47,720
slowly and ever so intentionally.

401
00:30:47,720 --> 00:30:50,500
St. Augustine, an early church father,

402
00:30:50,500 --> 00:30:51,780
says this in a sermon

403
00:30:51,780 --> 00:30:55,360
that he preached sometime in the early fifth century.

404
00:30:55,800 --> 00:30:56,240
He said,

405
00:30:56,240 --> 00:31:00,180
let the sinner not be ashamed to confess

406
00:31:00,180 --> 00:31:03,780
since God did not disdain to make himself a man.

407
00:31:03,780 --> 00:31:06,860
To bring this into context for today,

408
00:31:06,860 --> 00:31:09,420
this is well before,

409
00:31:09,420 --> 00:31:11,600
arguably well before

410
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:15,300
systematic confessional practices

411
00:31:15,300 --> 00:31:16,880
were made in the Catholic church.

412
00:31:16,880 --> 00:31:19,660
That didn't develop until the medieval period.

413
00:31:19,660 --> 00:31:21,560
This was way before.

414
00:31:21,560 --> 00:31:24,440
So St. Augustine believed very strongly

415
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:26,240
in confession to God,

416
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:30,100
in confession to church and community,

417
00:31:30,100 --> 00:31:35,840
not just going to a man in a booth to confess sin.

418
00:31:35,840 --> 00:31:38,720
It was the church and community.

419
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:41,120
The writer James tells us

420
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:43,360
that we should confess our sins to one another

421
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:44,840
and pray for one another

422
00:31:44,840 --> 00:31:48,360
that you will be healed in James 5, 16.

423
00:31:48,680 --> 00:31:50,620
Notice his words to one another,

424
00:31:50,620 --> 00:31:52,140
not just to God.

425
00:31:52,140 --> 00:31:54,580
Not in the privacy of our mind,

426
00:31:54,580 --> 00:31:57,820
although we should be having practiced private confession

427
00:31:57,820 --> 00:32:00,500
as well as with somebody who's trusted

428
00:32:00,500 --> 00:32:02,560
so that we can be healed,

429
00:32:02,560 --> 00:32:05,200
not just forgiven, but healed.

430
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:08,660
And James seems to even be echoing Jesus' words

431
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:11,180
about unforgiveness in Matthew 6,

432
00:32:11,180 --> 00:32:12,500
in the Lord's Prayer.

433
00:32:13,500 --> 00:32:16,440
Not only does the discipline of confession

434
00:32:16,440 --> 00:32:18,780
set our hearts free from sin and shame,

435
00:32:18,780 --> 00:32:20,480
it connects us to God,

436
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:22,060
it connects us to each other.

437
00:32:22,060 --> 00:32:25,360
Sin and shame are the great detractors.

438
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:28,820
They create distance between us and God,

439
00:32:28,820 --> 00:32:30,760
between us and each other.

440
00:32:30,760 --> 00:32:33,940
Confession brings us back together.

441
00:32:33,940 --> 00:32:36,020
So living in community

442
00:32:36,020 --> 00:32:38,660
in our time of individualism in the West

443
00:32:38,660 --> 00:32:40,000
is not easy.

444
00:32:40,700 --> 00:32:42,780
We face all sorts of challenges

445
00:32:42,780 --> 00:32:44,340
that we've already mentioned

446
00:32:44,340 --> 00:32:46,120
from busyness to flakiness and more.

447
00:32:46,120 --> 00:32:48,840
And one of the underlying problems

448
00:32:48,840 --> 00:32:50,580
that we face is shame.

449
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:55,980
And it's through the discipline of confession

450
00:32:55,980 --> 00:32:58,600
that we come out of the shadows

451
00:32:58,600 --> 00:33:00,180
into the light

452
00:33:00,180 --> 00:33:02,340
and we find healing.

453
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:06,560
We can't live 100% without sin.

454
00:33:06,560 --> 00:33:09,960
But we can live without shame.

455
00:33:09,960 --> 00:33:13,340
You can come out of hiding

456
00:33:13,340 --> 00:33:17,380
and you can know and be known.

457
00:33:17,380 --> 00:33:19,900
You can reconnect to God and others.

458
00:33:19,900 --> 00:33:21,460
And in doing so,

459
00:33:21,460 --> 00:33:24,140
we can increasingly year over year

460
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:26,040
become more and more free

461
00:33:26,040 --> 00:33:29,080
from that maybe that one sin

462
00:33:29,080 --> 00:33:30,400
that so entangled us.

463
00:33:30,400 --> 00:33:33,600
But we will find the deeper we go,

464
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:34,700
there's some other things

465
00:33:34,700 --> 00:33:36,920
that we still have to unravel

466
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:39,400
the closer that we get with God.

467
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:41,340
Tim Keller says this,

468
00:33:41,340 --> 00:33:44,160
to be loved but not known

469
00:33:44,160 --> 00:33:45,720
is comforting but superficial.

470
00:33:45,720 --> 00:33:48,780
